1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,240
The sixteenth century BC.

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,360
Babylon, the most famous city in the

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,720
world, had been sacked
and left without a ruler.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,080
The people who took the throne were
neither Sumerians nor Akkadians.

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,720
They had come down from the

6
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:14,560
Zagros mountains, a people
known for their horses.

7
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,440
They ruled Babylon for more
than four hundred years.

8
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:20,453
It was the longest dynasty
in Babylonian history.

9
00:00:20,493 --> 00:00:22,600
They exchanged letters
with the pharaohs of Egypt,

10
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:26,040
gave daughters to their kings,
and asked them for gold.

11
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:27,960
Their name was the Kassites.

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,460
In other words, the ancient
ancestors of the Kurds.

13
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:32,970
Welcome to the Dîrok channel.

14
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,840
The homeland of the Kassites was
the central Zagros mountains,
rising east of the Tigris River.

15
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:53,640
Today, Kurds and Lurs live in this region.

16
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:58,640
The Kassites first appear in history in
the time of Samsu-iluna, son of Hammurabi,

17
00:00:58,680 --> 00:00:59,827
king of Babylon.

18
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:02,920
In the Babylonian annals, one
year was named like this: the

19
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,027
year the Kassite army was defeated.

20
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:08,973
So even then, the Kassites stood
at the gates of Mesopotamia.

21
00:01:09,013 --> 00:01:11,280
The greatest strength of the
Kassites was their horses.

22
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,920
The horses and war chariots they
raised on the plateaus of the Zagros

23
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,907
made them one of the
fastest armies of their age.

24
00:01:17,947 --> 00:01:21,920
Many terms for horse breeding in
Babylonian texts came from the

25
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:23,020
Kassite language.

26
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:33,540
In 1595 BC, the Hittite king Mursili the
First marched all the way to Babylon and

27
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:34,660
sacked the city.

28
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:36,980
The dynasty founded by
Hammurabi collapsed.

29
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:40,073
The Hittites went home, and
Babylon was left without a ruler.

30
00:01:40,113 --> 00:01:42,393
It was the Kassites who filled this void.

31
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:46,740
Kassite kings took the throne of Babylon
and ruled for about four hundred and thirty

32
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:47,340
years.

33
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,740
The Babylonian King List counts
thirty-six Kassite kings.

34
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:55,220
It is the longest reign of a single
dynasty in the history of Mesopotamia.

35
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:58,020
The Kassites also gave the
country their own name.

36
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,100
In their time, Babylon
was known as Karduniash.

37
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:05,580
Some Kurdish writers see in this name
the traces of the names Kardu and Kurd.

38
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:07,380
The Kassites did not destroy Babylon.

39
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,620
On the contrary, they united scattered
city-states into a single country

40
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:15,480
and ruled southern Mesopotamia
in peace for centuries.

41
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:19,930
The Kassite king Agum recorded in
his inscription that he brought
back the statue of Marduk, which

42
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:21,957
the Hittites had carried off from Babylon.

43
00:02:21,997 --> 00:02:24,910
In this way, he won the hearts
of the people of Babylon.

44
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:34,010
In the fourteenth century BC, the
world was in the hands of five great

45
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:38,170
powers: Egypt, the Hittites,
Mitanni, Assyria and Kassite Babylon.

46
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:41,490
These kings addressed
one another as brother.

47
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:45,450
The Amarna Letters, found in Egypt, have
preserved this diplomacy to this day.

48
00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:47,170
In a letter to the pharaoh

49
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:50,330
Akhenaten, the Kassite king Burnaburiash
complains: I put the forty minas

50
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:53,170
of gold you sent into the furnace, and not

51
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:54,770
even ten minas came out.

52
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:58,637
The Kassite kings formed marriage
alliances with the pharaohs of Egypt.

53
00:02:58,677 --> 00:03:02,810
Kadashman-Enlil sent his daughter as a
bride to Pharaoh Amenhotep the Third.

54
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:07,330
This people who came down from
the Zagros now sat at the same
table as the most powerful kings

55
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:08,470
in the world.

56
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:10,600
The trade network of Kassite

57
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:15,320
Babylon stretched from the lapis lazuli
mines of Afghanistan to Dilmun in the

58
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:16,280
Persian Gulf.

59
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:20,300
Dilmun, today's Bahrain, was
governed by a Kassite governor.

60
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,560
The Kassites became the guardians
of Mesopotamian civilization.

61
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:33,480
King Kurigalzu founded the city of
Dur-Kurigalzu, bearing his own name, near

62
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:34,520
today's Baghdad.

63
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:37,440
The ziggurat of this
city still stands today.

64
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:41,920
The Kassites rebuilt the temples of
ancient cities such as Nippur and

65
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:42,520
Uruk.

66
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:47,320
The standard texts of great works
such as the Epic of Gilgamesh
were compiled in this period.

67
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:50,600
The Kassites also brought
a new tradition to

68
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:52,840
Mesopotamia: boundary
stones called kudurru.

69
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:56,120
Kings had the lands they granted
inscribed on these stones,

70
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:59,173
with the symbols of the
gods carved upon them.

71
00:03:59,213 --> 00:04:02,200
The Kassites honored the
gods of Babylon, but they

72
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:04,480
did not forget their own mountain gods.

73
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:08,980
Shuqamuna and Shumaliya were the
guardian gods of the Zagros mountains.

74
00:04:09,070 --> 00:04:13,270
Thousands of tablets from the Kassite period
were found in the excavations at Nippur.

75
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:17,870
These tablets describe daily life,
from tax records to the care of horses.

76
00:04:17,910 --> 00:04:21,050
Kassite craftsmen also mastered
the art of glassmaking.

77
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:30,590
In the twelfth century BC, Elam, the
neighbor to the east, went on the attack.

78
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:35,710
The Elamite king Shutruk-Nahhunte
sacked Babylon and carried the
famous stele inscribed with the Laws

79
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:38,470
of Hammurabi off to the city of Susa.

80
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:42,190
That stele is displayed today
in the Louvre Museum in Paris.

81
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:47,590
Around 1155 BC, the last Kassite
king, Enlil-nadin-ahi, lost his

82
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:48,390
throne.

83
00:04:48,430 --> 00:04:51,330
The Kassite dynasty in
Babylon came to an end.

84
00:04:56,050 --> 00:04:57,890
But the Kassites did not disappear.

85
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:02,130
Having lost the throne of
Babylon, they withdrew to their
homeland, the Zagros mountains.

86
00:05:02,170 --> 00:05:06,530
For centuries, Assyrian kings led
campaigns into the Kassite land.

87
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:11,290
The Assyrian king Sennacherib
recorded in his inscriptions
that in 702 BC he marched into

88
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:13,330
the mountain homeland of the Kassites.

89
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:18,010
A thousand years later, Greek
historians wrote of the same
people under the name Kossaioi.

90
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:21,290
Strabo writes that the
Kossaioi were skilled archers.

91
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:25,370
Arrian tells how Alexander the
Great led a winter campaign against

92
00:05:25,410 --> 00:05:26,130
them.

93
00:05:26,170 --> 00:05:30,330
Even the Persian kings paid the
Kossaioi a toll when crossing the

94
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:32,570
mountain road between Susa and Ecbatana.

95
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:36,030
Once again, the Zagros mountains
had protected a people.

96
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:41,040
Today, the Kurdish and Lur tribes living
in the same valleys of the Zagros carry

97
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:44,260
on this mountain life that has
lasted for thousands of years.

98
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,640
The Kassites were not
alone in these lands.

99
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:54,120
The Gutians, Lullubi, Hurrians, Mitanni

100
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:58,573
and Medes also lived to the
north and east of Mesopotamia.

101
00:05:58,613 --> 00:06:02,920
According to many historians,
these peoples are the ancient
layers that formed the Kurds.

102
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:06,440
Foremost among those who hold
this view are foreign scholars.

103
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:09,000
The American Assyriologist
Ephraim Speiser, in

104
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:14,200
his work Mesopotamian Origins,
counts the Kassites, together
with the Gutians and the Lullubi,

105
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:16,680
among the native peoples of the Zagros.

106
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:21,480
Arshak Safrastian, in his book Kurds and
Kurdistan, examines the peoples of the Zagros

107
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:23,613
as the ancestors of the Kurds.

108
00:06:23,653 --> 00:06:28,200
Mehrdad Izady presents the Kassites as
one of the most important layers forming

109
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:32,440
the Kurdish people, and looks for the
traces of Kassite mountain traditions in

110
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:33,587
Kurdish society today.

111
00:06:33,627 --> 00:06:37,960
The Kurdish historian Muhammad Amin
Zaki Beg also counts the Kassites
among the ancient ancestors

112
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,080
of the Kurds.

113
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,187
Of course, there are
opposing views as well.

114
00:06:41,227 --> 00:06:45,320
The main reason is that very few documents
in the Kassite language have survived.

115
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:49,080
All we have is a bilingual
glossary and the names of kings.

116
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:53,613
Yet geography, mountain life and a
continuity of thousands of years make this

117
00:06:53,653 --> 00:06:54,653
bond strong.

118
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:57,060
A people who came down
from the Zagros mountains

119
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:00,660
ruled Babylon, the most famous city
in the world, for four hundred years.

120
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:02,740
They sat at the same table as the

121
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:07,220
pharaohs, protected the civilization
of Mesopotamia, and in the end
returned once more to their mountains.

122
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:11,700
The story of the Kassites is one of
the oldest pages of Kurdish history.

123
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:16,020
If you enjoyed this video, don't forget
to subscribe and share your thoughts in

124
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:17,060
the comments.
